parts of yellowstone national park may  be closed for the rest of the summer  season after historic flooding  overwhelmed bridges and roads forcing  more than 10 thousand visitors to be  evacuated some of those trying to escape  the water nearly got hit by falling  rocks scary stuff jonathan vigliotti has  the story of one family who saw their  home get swept away  oh it just moved part of the bank just  slivers just started coming off and we  knew immediately we need to go right now  victoria and tj britton ran out of their  front door monday with a few belongings  and their pets not knowing if they would  ever return within hours they watched  from higher ground as their deck then  garage  then entire home was swallowed by the  rushing waters of the yellowstone river  it's like watching a lot of funerals  i've watched this on tv happen to lots  of other people and when it happens to  you it's devastating officials say it  floated for five miles before sinking  we're lucky it held out until the  morning and we were able to have a  little heads up and get out of there  because otherwise  we would probably be swimming in the  river across montana there are water  rescues as the flooding is stranding  families and isolating communities we've  been spending most of the day today  helping people that have been stranded  the river which carries its way through  yellowstone national park hit historic  water levels after days of rain and  large snow melt  usually a destination for more than 2  million visitors each summer the  sprawling 2.2 million acre park is now  evacuated and completely shut down as  mudslides rocks and raging water tore  through roads and bridges  the park could remain closed for up to a  week and officials say some parts won't  open for a substantial length of time  because of damaged infrastructure  for cbs mornings i'm jonathan vigliotti
 

